\title{Certified Adversarial Robustness via Sheaf Cohomology}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-30}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a topological account of certified adversarial robustness for classifiers that are piecewise linear over their input space. Two ingredients are isolated and shown to be independent. The first is a \emph{local} (stalk) certificate: for a linear score $s_w(x) = \sum_i w_i x_i$ measured against $\Linf$ perturbations, the exact Lipschitz constant is the weight $L^1$ norm $\norm{w}_1$, and a point $x_0$ whose margin satisfies $\norm{w}_1 \cdot r < |s_w(x_0)|$ has its predicted sign invariant on the entire $\Linf$ ball of radius $r$; the tight certified radius is $|s_w(x_0)|/\norm{w}_1$. The second is a \emph{global} gluing law governed by the first \v{C}ech cohomology of the nerve of the region cover. On a tree-shaped (path) nerve the first cohomology vanishes---every prescribed overlap discrepancy is the coboundary of a global potential---so local certificates always assemble into one global certificate. On a loop-shaped (cyclic) nerve the first cohomology is nontrivial: the unit discrepancy cochain has loop holonomy $n+1 \ne 0$ and is not a coboundary, an ineliminable obstruction we identify as the cohomological signature of an adversarial cycle. Our main theorem combines the two: on a tree cover with a uniform per-region margin, every region is $\Linf$-stable within a common radius $R$ and every overlap discrepancy glues. We conclude that certification factors as \emph{stalk margin} $\times$ \emph{nerve acyclicity}, and that these two failure modes are logically independent.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A binary classifier is \emph{certifiably robust} at an input $x_0$ with radius $R$ if its output label is provably constant on the ball of radius $R$ about $x_0$. For high-stakes deployments such certificates are far more valuable than empirical accuracy against known attacks, because they rule out \emph{all} perturbations within the radius, including attacks not yet invented.

For a single linear score the certified radius is classical and exact. The difficulty is \emph{globalization}. A piecewise-linear network (for instance, one built from rectified-linear units) partitions its input domain into activation regions; inside each region the network is exactly affine, hence governed by a local linear score and a local certificate. The regions overlap, and an honest global certificate requires the local certificates to be \emph{mutually consistent on overlaps} and to \emph{assemble into a single global section}. This is precisely the situation that sheaf theory and \v{C}ech cohomology were built to analyze: local data, restriction to overlaps, and the obstruction to gluing.

This paper makes the analogy exact and provable in the simplest faithful setting. We prove (i) the tight $\Linf$ stalk certificate, (ii) vanishing of first cohomology on tree-shaped nerves with an explicit gluing primitive, and (iii) nonvanishing of first cohomology on loop-shaped nerves via an explicit non-coboundary. We then combine (i) and (ii) into a single global certification theorem and contrast it with the cyclic obstruction. The conceptual payoff is a clean factorization of robustness into an independent local axis (margin) and global axis (cover topology).

\section{Setup and definitions}

Throughout, the input space is $\R^d$ with the $\Linf$ metric $\norm{x}_\infty = \max_i |x_i|$.

\begin{definition}[Linear score and classifier]
Given a weight vector $w \in \R^d$, the \emph{linear score} is
\[ s_w(x) = \sum_{i=1}^d w_i x_i. \]
The associated binary classifier outputs the sign of $s_w(x)$; we encode the label by the predicate $[\,s_w(x) > 0\,]$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Weight $L^1$ norm]
$\norm{w}_1 = \sum_{i=1}^d |w_i|$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Margin]
The \emph{margin} of $w$ at a reference point $x_0$ is $|s_w(x_0)|$, the distance of the score from the decision threshold $0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\Linf$ ball]
For radius $r \ge 0$, the closed $\Linf$ ball about $x_0$ is $\{x : |x_i - x_{0,i}| \le r \text{ for all } i\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cover and nerve]
A finite open cover $\{U_0, \dots, U_n\}$ of the input domain has a \emph{nerve}, the abstract simplicial complex whose $k$-simplices are the $(k{+}1)$-fold nonempty intersections of cover elements. We work with two combinatorial shapes:
\begin{itemize}
\item the \emph{path nerve}, with vertices $0, \dots, n$ and edges $\{i, i{+}1\}$ for $0 \le i < n$ (a tree);
\item the \emph{loop nerve} (cyclic nerve), with the same vertices and edges plus the closing edge $\{n, 0\}$ (a single cycle).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cochains and coboundaries]
A \emph{$0$-cochain} assigns a real number to each vertex (region); a \emph{$1$-cochain} assigns a real number to each edge (overlap). On the path nerve the coboundary operator $\delta^0$ sends a $0$-cochain $f$ to the $1$-cochain
\[ (\delta^0 f)_i = f_{i+1} - f_i \qquad (0 \le i < n). \]
On the loop nerve the cyclic coboundary $\delta^{\mathrm{cyc}}$ adds the closing edge:
\[ (\delta^{\mathrm{cyc}} f)_i = f_{i+1 \bmod (n+1)} - f_i \qquad (0 \le i \le n). \]
A $1$-cochain $g$ is a \emph{coboundary} if $g = \delta f$ for some $0$-cochain $f$; the \emph{first cohomology} $H^1$ is the quotient of $1$-cochains by coboundaries. In these one-dimensional nerves all $1$-cochains are cocycles, so $H^1$ measures exactly the failure to be a coboundary.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Holonomy]
On the loop nerve, the \emph{holonomy} of a $1$-cochain $g$ is its total around the cycle, $\sum_{i=0}^{n} g_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Interpretation]
A $0$-cochain is a candidate \emph{global potential}---one consistent scalar reconciliation per region. A $1$-cochain is a prescribed pattern of \emph{overlap discrepancies} between neighboring local certificates. Gluing the local certificates is exactly the problem of realizing a given $1$-cochain as a coboundary $\delta f$.
\end{remark}

\section{The stalk certificate: exact $\Linf$ robustness of a linear score}

\begin{lemma}[$\Linf$ Lipschitz bound, tight]\label{lem:lip}
For all $x, y \in \R^d$ and $r \ge 0$, if $|x_i - y_i| \le r$ for every $i$, then
\[ |s_w(x) - s_w(y)| \le \norm{w}_1 \cdot r. \]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By linearity $s_w(x) - s_w(y) = \sum_i w_i (x_i - y_i)$. The triangle inequality for finite sums gives $|s_w(x) - s_w(y)| \le \sum_i |w_i|\,|x_i - y_i|$. Bounding each $|x_i - y_i| \le r$ and factoring yields $\sum_i |w_i|\, r = \norm{w}_1 r$.
\end{proof}

The constant is the dual-norm pairing: the dual of $\norm{\cdot}_\infty$ is $\norm{\cdot}_1$, and equality is attained by the perturbation $\Delta x_i = r \cdot \sign(w_i)$, so $\norm{w}_1$ is the \emph{exact} Lipschitz constant, not merely an upper bound.

\begin{theorem}[Sign stability / stalk certificate]\label{thm:stalk}
Fix $w$, a reference $x_0$, and $r \ge 0$ with the strict margin condition
\[ \norm{w}_1 \cdot r < |s_w(x_0)|. \]
Then for every $x$ in the $\Linf$ ball of radius $r$ about $x_0$, $s_w(x)$ has the same sign as $s_w(x_0)$; in particular the predicate $[\,s_w(x) > 0\,]$ is constant on the ball.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:lip}, $|s_w(x) - s_w(x_0)| \le \norm{w}_1 r < |s_w(x_0)|$. If $s_w(x_0) > 0$ then $s_w(x) > s_w(x_0) - |s_w(x_0)| = 0$; if $s_w(x_0) < 0$ then $s_w(x) < s_w(x_0) + |s_w(x_0)| = 0$. Either way the sign is preserved, equivalently $[\,s_w(x) > 0\,] = [\,s_w(x_0) > 0\,]$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Certified radius and positivity]\label{cor:radius}
If $|s_w(x_0)| > 0$ and $\norm{w}_1 > 0$, the largest radius certified by Theorem~\ref{thm:stalk} is
\[ R = \frac{|s_w(x_0)|}{\norm{w}_1} > 0, \]
and this radius is strictly positive. Any $r < R$ satisfies the margin condition and is therefore certified.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The condition $\norm{w}_1 r < |s_w(x_0)|$ is equivalent to $r < |s_w(x_0)|/\norm{w}_1$ since $\norm{w}_1 > 0$. Positivity of the quotient follows from positivity of numerator and denominator.
\end{proof}

We call $R = |s_w(x_0)|/\norm{w}_1$ the \emph{stalk radius} of the region. The certificate is intrinsically \emph{local}: it guarantees a ball about one reference point, with no claim about how neighboring regions' balls interact.

\section{The gluing law: first cohomology of the nerve}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing $H^1$ on the path nerve]\label{thm:tree}
On the path nerve with vertices $0, \dots, n$ and edges $\{i, i{+}1\}$, the coboundary $\delta^0$ is surjective: for every $1$-cochain $g$ there exists a $0$-cochain $f$ with $\delta^0 f = g$. Hence $H^1(\text{path nerve}) = 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Define $f$ by partial sums: $f_0 = 0$ and, for $1 \le k \le n$, $f_k = \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} g_i$. Then for each edge $i$, $(\delta^0 f)_i = f_{i+1} - f_i = g_i$ by telescoping. Thus $\delta^0 f = g$. Since every $1$-cochain is a coboundary, the quotient defining $H^1$ is trivial.
\end{proof}

The map $g \mapsto f$ is an explicit \emph{gluing primitive}: it reconciles any prescribed overlap discrepancy on a tree-shaped cover. The surjectivity is genuine, not vacuous---a global potential is produced for every input.

\begin{theorem}[Nonvanishing $H^1$ on the loop nerve]\label{thm:loop}
On the loop nerve of $n+1$ regions, a $1$-cochain $g$ is a coboundary $\delta^{\mathrm{cyc}} f$ only if its holonomy $\sum_{i=0}^{n} g_i$ vanishes. Consequently the unit cochain $g \equiv 1$, whose holonomy equals $n + 1 \neq 0$, is \emph{not} a coboundary, and $H^1(\text{loop nerve}) \neq 0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For any $0$-cochain $f$, summing the cyclic coboundary around the loop telescopes:
\[ \sum_{i=0}^{n} (\delta^{\mathrm{cyc}} f)_i = \sum_{i=0}^{n} \big(f_{i+1 \bmod (n+1)} - f_i\big) = 0, \]
because each value $f_j$ appears once with a $+$ sign and once with a $-$ sign. Hence any coboundary has zero holonomy. The constant cochain $1$ has holonomy $\sum_{i=0}^{n} 1 = n + 1 > 0$, so it cannot be a coboundary; its class is nonzero in $H^1$.
\end{proof}

The holonomy is a single scalar that completely captures the obstruction on a one-cycle nerve: a loop $1$-cochain glues if and only if its holonomy is zero, and the residual holonomy is the nonzero cohomology class.

\section{Main results: the global certificate and its obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Global certification on a tree cover]\label{thm:global}
Let the input domain be covered by activation regions $0, \dots, n$ arranged in a path nerve, region $i$ governed by a linear score $s_{w_i}$ with reference point $x_0^{(i)}$. Fix a common radius $R \ge 0$ and suppose the uniform margin condition holds:
\[ \norm{w_i}_1 \cdot R < |s_{w_i}(x_0^{(i)})| \qquad \text{for every region } i. \]
Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{(Stalk certificates.)} For every region $i$ and every $x$ in the $\Linf$ ball of radius $R$ about $x_0^{(i)}$, the prediction is unchanged: $[\,s_{w_i}(x) > 0\,] = [\,s_{w_i}(x_0^{(i)}) > 0\,]$.
\item \textbf{(Gluing / vanishing $H^1$.)} Every $1$-cochain $g$ of overlap discrepancies admits a global potential $f$ with $\delta^0 f = g$.
\end{enumerate}
The certified global radius is therefore $R$, valid simultaneously on every region.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Part 1 is Theorem~\ref{thm:stalk} applied region by region with the uniform margin hypothesis. Part 2 is Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}. The two parts are independent and together certify radius $R$ globally.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cyclic cover hosts an unremovable obstruction]\label{thm:cyclic}
On the loop nerve of $n+1$ regions, there is no global potential $f$ with $\delta^{\mathrm{cyc}} f \equiv 1$; i.e. the unit overlap-discrepancy cochain cannot be glued away. This nonzero first-cohomology class, of holonomy $n+1$, is the cohomological signature of an adversarial cycle.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:loop}: the holonomy $n+1 \neq 0$ rules out any coboundary representation.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factorization of certified robustness]\label{thm:factor}
A global certificate of radius $R$ exists when (a) every stalk clears the margin $\norm{w_i}_1 R < |s_{w_i}(x_0^{(i)})|$ \emph{and} (b) the nerve of the cover is acyclic ($H^1 = 0$). The two conditions are independent: (a) is a per-region (local) quantity blind to cover topology, while (b) is a global topological quantity blind to any single region's margin.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
The certified radius factors conceptually as
\[ \text{global certificate} \;=\; (\text{stalk margin}) \,\times\, (\text{nerve acyclicity}). \]
The surviving logical implication is \emph{vanishing $H^1 \Rightarrow$ glueability}. The converse fails: a tree cover (with $H^1 = 0$) can still host a vulnerable point if some stalk margin is too small. Cohomology controls \emph{gluing}, never the stalk margin. Thus margin-maximizing training, which acts only on axis (a), cannot by itself remove vulnerabilities that live on axis (b).
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (Stalk certified radius).} Given $w$ and $x_0$, compute the score $s = \sum_i w_i x_{0,i}$, the norm $N = \sum_i |w_i|$, and return $R = |s|/N$ (with $R = +\infty$ if $N = 0$ and $s \neq 0$, and $R = 0$ if $s = 0$). Complexity $O(d)$. Correctness is Corollary~\ref{cor:radius}.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (Tree gluing primitive).} Given a $1$-cochain $g = (g_0, \dots, g_{n-1})$ on a path nerve, return the partial-sum potential $f$ with $f_0 = 0$, $f_{k} = f_{k-1} + g_{k-1}$. Then $\delta^0 f = g$ exactly. Complexity $O(n)$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:tree}.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (Holonomy obstruction test).} Given a $1$-cochain $g$ on a loop nerve, return the holonomy $h = \sum_{i=0}^{n} g_i$. The cochain glues iff $h = 0$; otherwise $h$ is the irreducible obstruction. Complexity $O(n)$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:loop}.

\paragraph{Algorithm D (Global certificate on a tree cover).} Given per-region $(w_i, x_0^{(i)})$ and a candidate $R$: verify $\norm{w_i}_1 R < |s_{w_i}(x_0^{(i)})|$ for all $i$ (each $O(d)$); if all pass, accept $R$ as a global certified radius and emit the tree gluing primitive for any required overlap reconciliation. Complexity $O(nd)$. Correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:global}.

\section{Applications}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Combining per-region certificates.} Certified-defense pipelines that already decompose a network into linear regions can use Theorem~\ref{thm:global} to fuse per-region certificates into one global radius equal to the minimum stalk radius---provided the region-adjacency nerve is acyclic.
\item \textbf{Cover refinement as a defense.} Theorem~\ref{thm:cyclic} identifies cycles as the obstruction. Spanning-tree sparsification of the region-adjacency graph removes cycles cheaply, converting fragile local guarantees into a global guarantee (Theorem~\ref{thm:global}).
\item \textbf{Diagnosing residual vulnerability.} When margin-maximizing training leaves a model attackable, Theorem~\ref{thm:factor} predicts the residual weakness lives in cover topology (nonzero holonomy), not in the margins---a falsifiable, actionable diagnosis.
\end{itemize}

\section{Discussion and limitations}

The framework is deliberately minimal: one-dimensional nerves (path and loop) make the cohomology computation transparent and the obstruction a single scalar. The honest scope is the implication \emph{acyclicity $\Rightarrow$ glueability}; we do \emph{not} claim \emph{vulnerability $\Rightarrow$ nonzero cohomology}, which is false in general (a tree cover with a thin stalk is vulnerable yet acyclic). The two axes---stalk margin and nerve cohomology---are genuinely independent, which is both the central insight and the principal caveat: hardening a model requires acting on both.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{(1) Holonomy lower-bounds the adversarial budget.} For a piecewise-linear classifier whose activation regions form a loop, the smallest perturbation flipping the prediction around the loop should be bounded below by the loop's holonomy divided by the largest local sensitivity; the certified radius around the whole loop is then governed by a single first-cohomology class. The inconsistency of local certificates around a cycle is concentrated in one scalar---the holonomy---so the hardest adversarial direction is the one that integrates this class around the loop.

\paragraph{(2) Acyclic covers admit a uniform global certificate.} Whenever the nerve is acyclic, the per-region certified radii should assemble into a global certified radius equal to their minimum, with no loss from gluing---vanishing first cohomology turns the family of local certificates into a genuine global section, making certification a worst-case minimum rather than a fragile chain of overlap compatibilities. Cycle-removing cover refinements (spanning-tree sparsification of region-adjacency graphs) are a cheap, testable engineering lever.

\paragraph{(3) Cohomology controls gluing, margin controls stalks---and they are independent.} Robustness failure should factor into two independent causes: a small stalk margin (local, invisible to nerve cohomology) and a nonzero nerve holonomy (global, invisible to any single region). Neither implies the other, and a classifier is globally robust iff both vanish at the relevant scale. Empirically, margin-maximizing training still leaves models vulnerable; the conjecture predicts the residual vulnerability lives in cover topology.

\end{document}

